WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Before the title, look at the image. It is
a real photograph of Earth by the EPIC camera

2
00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:40.000
on DSCOVR (7 Oct 2026), about 1.5 million km
away. NASA and ESA publish these views for free.

3
00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:30.000
This hour: learn to read them — what you
see, how it was made, what decision it allows.

4
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:56.667
Three stops map the hour: (1) from image to
decision — GEOINT and how satellites look; (2) real

5
00:02:56.667 --> 00:03:23.333
cases — fire, water, forest, ice, ocean, hurricane; (3)
open NASA and ESA data, Landsat vs Sentinel, and

6
00:03:23.333 --> 00:03:50.000
a live map in the browser.

7
00:03:50.000 --> 00:04:10.000
First stop: understand. Mixed audience is fine. Same path
every image: what an untrained eye sees, how the

8
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:30.000
satellite built the view, then the decision it enables.
That path is geospatial intelligence on already published data.

9
00:04:30.000 --> 00:05:45.000
GEOINT: image + location + analysis = decision. Image
is raw material; location ties every pixel to Earth;

10
00:05:45.000 --> 00:07:00.000
analysis translates what green means; decision is what we
do. Example: where do crops need water this week?

11
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:56.667
Optical is like a phone camera — needs sunlight;
clouds and night block the ground. Radar is like

12
00:07:56.667 --> 00:08:53.333
an echo — own microwave signal works at night
and through clouds. Landsat/Sentinel-2 for how it looks; Sentinel-1/NISAR

13
00:08:53.333 --> 00:09:50.000
for water through clouds.

14
00:09:50.000 --> 00:11:07.500
Four instruments, four questions: optical (what color?), thermal (where
is extra heat?), radar (did the surface change?), lidar

15
00:11:07.500 --> 00:12:25.000
(how tall?). Camera, thermometer, echo, ruler. Pick one question for Space Apps.

16
00:12:25.000 --> 00:14:00.000
Same Nile Delta, same day (12 Jul 2024), MODIS
Terra: true color vs near-infrared false color. Healthy leaves

17
00:14:00.000 --> 00:15:35.000
bounce NIR; painting it green separates plants
from soil. Available in NASA Worldview / GIBS.

18
00:15:35.000 --> 00:17:10.000
Three circles = the full path on the same
delta: photo → false color → NDVI (a painted

19
00:17:10.000 --> 00:18:45.000
number). Cite dates: July 2024 for photo/false color; 8-day NDVI composite July 2025.

20
00:18:45.000 --> 00:19:25.000
Everything that follows is real data. The
method does not change: image first, conclusion second.

21
00:19:25.000 --> 00:20:50.000
Two layers in one view: smoke is the photograph;
red points are heat detections placed with coordinates (FIRMS

22
00:20:50.000 --> 00:22:15.000
/ VIIRS).

23
00:22:15.000 --> 00:23:45.000
Read the color code first, then before/after. Electric green
is vegetation; dark blue is water; pale cyan is

24
00:23:45.000 --> 00:25:15.000
cloud.

25
00:25:15.000 --> 00:26:30.000
Dark green is forest; light brown is open soil.
A white cloud in year 2000 is not a

26
00:26:30.000 --> 00:27:45.000
clearing.

27
00:27:45.000 --> 00:28:52.500
One portrait, not a before/after. White is ice; turquoise
is lakes. The memory since 1972 is the method

28
00:28:52.500 --> 00:30:00.000
— and it lives in Landsat.

29
00:30:00.000 --> 00:31:07.500
In 2000 the south is a green water body.
In 2024 that same body is a pale desert.

30
00:31:07.500 --> 00:32:15.000
The smaller north still holds water.

31
00:32:15.000 --> 00:33:40.000
Black is land, not sea. Colors are only the
ocean — and they are the difference from normal,

32
00:33:40.000 --> 00:35:05.000
not the water’s true color.

33
00:35:05.000 --> 00:37:20.000
True-color photo. White is cloud. The tighter center
of the spiral is the eye. Land appears above.

34
00:37:20.000 --> 00:38:32.500
Same delta as before, at night. The brightest light
below is Cairo. Surrounding black is desert without lights

35
00:38:32.500 --> 00:39:45.000
— not a hole in the data.

36
00:39:45.000 --> 00:40:30.000
You already know how to read. Now learn where to reopen each scene.

37
00:40:30.000 --> 00:43:00.000
Three doors, all free, each owning a file type.
Create your account today: Earthdata, Landsat/EarthExplorer, Copernicus Data Space.

38
00:43:00.000 --> 00:45:20.000
Say it slowly: decades of change? Landsat. This neighborhood this month? Sentinel-2.

39
00:45:20.000 --> 00:47:40.000
You do not need the last step. The first already yields a citable figure.

40
00:47:40.000 --> 00:49:55.000
The demo teaches the gesture, not a perfect place:
location, layer, two dates, and say aloud what the

41
00:49:55.000 --> 00:52:10.000
color means.

42
00:52:10.000 --> 00:52:35.000
Do not open registrations live. This slide is for
the photo. The title is the logbook; below is

43
00:52:35.000 --> 00:53:00.000
the official step-by-step.

44
00:53:00.000 --> 00:55:30.000
Each idea rests on an image you already read. Choose one in silence.

45
00:55:30.000 --> 00:57:20.000
Start with the question. The figure caption
must carry sensor, date, color recipe, and source.

46
00:57:20.000 --> 00:59:00.000
Leave the link on screen about eight seconds for photos and notes.

47
00:59:00.000 --> 01:00:30.000
Read the final line slowly. If time
remains, one question. Hard stop at minute 60.

48
01:00:00.000 --> 01:30:58.000
Q&A and audience conversation. Captions for the main talk end here.
